WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.356
Module 6 is about MCP servers — the standard way to plug whole toolboxes from the outside world into your agent.

2
00:00:04.356 --> 00:00:07.000
In this one we connect a real one, and cover the two ways they talk.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.726
MCP — the Model Context Protocol — is an open standard for exposing tools to an AI.

4
00:00:09.726 --> 00:00:13.339
Instead of the app building every integration, anyone can ship an MCP server, and the app just connects to it.

5
00:00:13.339 --> 00:00:16.000
There are hundreds already: GitHub, databases, file systems, search, you name it.

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.758
Go to Automation, MCP Servers, Add server.

7
00:00:17.758 --> 00:00:19.977
There are two transports — two ways a server can run.

8
00:00:19.977 --> 00:00:24.121
Stdio, a local command the app launches; and http, a remote server you connect to over the network.

9
00:00:24.121 --> 00:00:25.000
Let's do one of each.

10
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.034
A stdio example: many servers run through npx, launched with an environment variable for a token — the GitHub server is a classic.

11
00:00:31.034 --> 00:00:36.000
You give the command, add the token as an env var, save, and the app spawns it locally and lists its tools.

12
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.381
An http example: a remote server you reach by URL.

13
00:00:38.381 --> 00:00:45.714
Many use OAuth — you click sign in, authorize in your browser, and the token comes back and is stored in your OS credential store, not in a settings file.

14
00:00:45.714 --> 00:00:47.000
Same as your provider keys.

15
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.638
The gotcha that saves a support ticket: for npx servers, run the exact command once in a real terminal first.

16
00:00:51.638 --> 00:00:56.745
The very first run downloads the package, which can take long enough to time out inside the app and look like a failure.

17
00:00:56.745 --> 00:00:59.000
Once it's cached, connecting from the app is instant.

18
00:00:59.000 --> 00:00:59.765
One server is easy.

19
00:00:59.765 --> 00:01:01.295
But what happens when you connect ten?

20
00:01:01.295 --> 00:01:05.000
Next episode explains the app's clever answer to not drowning the model in tool definitions.

